def add(title,kind='table',*,notes='',oldnote=None,refs=(),foot='',**kw):
 slides.append(dict(title=title,kind=kind,notes=old.get(oldnote,notes) if oldnote else notes,
                    sources=[sources[r] for r in refs],foot=foot,**kw))
def fig(name,standard=True):return 'reports/presentation/diagrams/'+('standard/' if standard else 'failures/')+name+'.png'
add('An Instructor-Configurable\nCourse Digital Twin','cover',oldnote=1,refs=['brief'],
 subtitle='Software design, experimental findings and remaining limitations',details='Hikaru\nResearch project presentation\n7 September 2026')
add('Delivery against the project brief',oldnote=2,refs=['brief','workflow','G','H'],foot='Implementation and verification have different acceptance scopes.',
 rows=[['Requested outcome','Implemented','Remaining acceptance gap'],['Course configuration','Reviewed materials and releases','Bounded local workflow only'],['Grounded support','Course scope and source citations','Fresh factual gates remain unmet'],['Teaching preferences','Approved profile enters generation','Actual-instructor fidelity untested'],['Continuing support','Persistent goals and scheduled work','Useful intervention unestablished'],['Instructor oversight','Review, pause and withdrawal','Full current composition needs evaluation']],widths=[270,430,460])
add('Thirty virtual days in the actual software','video',refs=['demo'],foot='Recording configuration: deterministic baseline. Synthetic users and time. Duration 4:06.',
 image='reports/generated/autonomous-film/review-18.png',
 notes='I will first show the software. There are two professors, two courses and four synthetic students. Watch what happens after a student stops typing. The system observes the saved state and processes due work as the virtual clock advances. Also watch the professor pause and the student consent change. The recording uses existing deterministic services in an isolated environment. It is a demonstration of operation, not the historical model-backed evaluation.\n\n[Play the embedded video. No speech for 4 minutes 6 seconds.]\n\nThe important point is that support can originate from the system. The remaining slides examine whether the designs behind that behavior are useful, correct and supported by valid evidence.')
add('Instructor settings and observed behavior','settings',refs=['workflow','profiles'],foot='Saved finding F21. This is a historical example, separate from the new demo.',
 rows=[['Setting','Where it acts'],['Approved sources and objectives','Evidence selection and goal scope'],['Action permissions and consent','Execution and delivery checks'],['Tone, depth and help sequence','Inputs to response generation']],
 quote='For “How does slot seal work”, what is your current explanation, and which step are you unsure about?',
 points=['The response elicits an attempt.','Concept-specific diagnosis remains absent.'],
 notes='The instructor settings reach different parts of the software. Source permissions define what evidence the tutor may use. Approved objectives define what its goals may cover. Action permissions and consent restrict whether the system can deliver a proposed action. These are executable controls. A response model cannot authorize itself to ignore them.\n\nTeaching preferences have a different role. Tone, explanation depth and the help sequence enter the generation pathway. Their presence in an input does not prove that the output follows the instructor’s practice. We need to inspect what the student actually receives.\n\nThis saved Socratic profile requested a diagnostic question, a student attempt and then one hint. The student asked how slot seal worked in the synthetic course protocol. The tutor asked for the student’s current explanation and the step they were unsure about. That shows part of the intended help sequence. However, it does not diagnose the particular conceptual difficulty. The response is still generic.\n\nA separate explanatory example uses a different topic and context. It is not a controlled profile comparison. For a stronger test, I would hold the question, evidence and learner state fixed, vary only the approved profile, and independently review the resulting instruction. That remains proposed work. The owner can inspect the settings and enforcement points now, but instructor fidelity still needs evidence.')
add('System architecture and comparison boundaries','figure',oldnote=6,refs=['runtime'],image=fig('02-c4-containers'),foot='Logical container view. The demo uses a local harness for worker ticks and synchronous ingestion.')
add('What the simulation executes and supplies','figure',refs=['G','E','demo'],image=fig('14-operational-components',False),foot='Study G executes software and model calls. Study E separately evaluates latent learner state and timing.',
 notes='There are three different kinds of evidence in this presentation, and I do not want to combine their claims. The video shows a new deterministic software demonstration. Its student inputs and thirty-day clock are controlled, while existing product services create goals, select actions and save deliveries.\n\nThe historical operational dialogue study shown in this component diagram also supplies synthetic student behavior and virtual elapsed time. However, it invokes the configured external model through actual tutoring and autonomy services. It checks saved turns, delivery, consent and restart behavior. The student driver decides attendance and constructs replies according to its configured probabilities. Those replies are not observations from real students.\n\nThe learner and timing study is separate. It contains a hidden simulated learner state for scoring predictions and intervention outcomes. It does not execute the text retrieval and generation pipeline. The candidates see permitted observations, while the evaluator can use hidden simulator state. That distinction is why a lower estimation error there does not demonstrate better instructional text in the product.\n\nIn a classroom, actual students would supply attendance, attempts and reactions. Here those inputs are simulated so that runs are finite and reproducible. The measured software actions are real executions, but the human behavior and educational outcome assumptions are supplied by the experimental design. I therefore attach each result to its own study rather than calling every thirty-day result the same experiment.')
add('Why the first factual designs rejected questions',oldnote=7,refs=['round1'],foot='Round 1: 495 development cases, 350 chunks, no external model. Boundary action 100% in all arms.',
 rows=[['Design tried','Added mechanism','Grounded','Answerable action'],['Lexical control','Any-hit acceptance','52.66%','100%'],['Evidence-first hierarchy','Whole-question coverage','24.81%','36.46%'],['Plan-observe manifest','Decomposition and observations','24.81%','36.20%']],widths=[315,395,200,250],
 points=['Both complex paths shared the same downstream coverage defect.','Question scaffolding became a requirement for evidence.'])
add('Typed targets helped; extra ranking did not',oldnote=8,refs=['A'],foot='Study A: one development fold, 397 answerable cases within 497 total cases.',
 rows=[['Candidate','Grounded answerable cases','p95 latency'],['Control before typed targets','253 / 397','—'],['Typed target and cardinality','355 / 397','1.36 ms'],['Typed target plus section ranking','355 / 397','2.79 ms']],widths=[560,350,250],
 points=['Making the required facts explicit improved this development result.','The extra ranking stage added latency without a quality gain.'])
add('Retrieval did not ensure complete answers',oldnote=9,refs=['B'],foot='Study B: fresh, source-disjoint 1,000 cases. Grounded gate 95%; boundary gate 98%.',
 rows=[['Actual-product arm','All evidence@3','Fully grounded','Boundary action'],['BM25 + dominance gate','98.00%','63.25%','96.00%'],['Qwen3 hybrid + same gate','92.88%','62.00%','95.00%']],widths=[490,215,220,235],
 points=['The retained fallback still missed the acceptance gates.','A correct region can still yield the wrong claim or an incomplete answer.'])
add('Planner alternatives and the reject-only failure',refs=['fold3','audit3','fold4'],foot='Fold 003 labels were later reinterpreted. Preferred-label agreement was not action validity.',
 rows=[['Design','Action-selection mechanism','Finding'],['A','Deterministic event rule','Retained as the control'],['B','Model proposal without lookahead','Did not justify replacing A'],['C','Proposal plus analytic lookahead','Mixed label and utility results'],['C + V','C followed by a rejecting verifier','Rejection supplies no replacement']],widths=[150,545,465],
 points=['A verifier could suppress an otherwise usable selected action.'],
 notes='The planner comparison changed the decision stages inside a common execution boundary. Every method still faced the same permissions, evidence checks and delivery rules. A used a deterministic event rule. B asked a model to propose a permitted action without lookahead. C added deterministic analytic lookahead to rank actions. C plus V added a model verifier after the C selection.\n\nThe question was whether these extra decision stages improved the resulting action enough to justify replacing the simple control. In the third development fold, the deterministic control had the best registered hidden utility. B’s direct proposal did not justify automatic priority over that control. C had stronger agreement with a preferred-action label, but lower utility than A. Those metrics asked different questions.\n\nThe additional verifier exposed a more concrete failure. It could reject a usable move, and the reject branch returned no action. It did not search for another useful move or return the baseline. This is a failure of the reject-only composition, not an argument that all verification is harmful.\n\nThe later audit also corrected the interpretation of acceptable-move scores. Preferred-label agreement had been confused with permitted transition validity. Five planner failures came from a non-authoritative reason-string length constraint. These details matter because they explain which boundary failed. They do not support saying that the entire safety mechanism collapsed. The successor therefore preserved the baseline when a proposed replacement did not earn acceptance.')
add('Guarded replacement retains the baseline','figure',refs=['fold4','C'],image=fig('11-guarded-replacement',False),foot='Study C, 1,000 contexts: A/H utility 0.7954/0.8002; preferred label 74%/73%; valid action 100%/100%.',
 notes='The H design changes what happens when a proposal is weak. It first computes the deterministic baseline. If there is no authorized evidence, the result is no action. When evidence is available, a model proposal can replace the baseline only if it is valid, permitted, agrees with the analytic best action and exceeds the minimum value margin of zero point zero four. Otherwise, the method keeps A.\n\nThis preserves a useful available move instead of allowing a reject-only stage to remove it without replacement. In the fresh fourth fold, all four methods produced valid actions. H had utility zero point seven eight eight zero compared with zero point seven eight six zero for A. The other methods did not dominate all metrics.\n\nIn the separate one-thousand-context confirmation, H again had a small positive paired utility improvement. The means were zero point eight zero zero two for H and zero point seven nine five four for A. Preferred-label agreement fell from seventy-four to seventy-three percent. The result supports conditional progression, not improvement on every measure.\n\nThere is still an important unresolved question. The analytic ranking, fallback and model proposal work together. This comparison does not isolate the value added by the language model itself. An analytic-only ablation would be needed. Also, this utility comes from a synthetic instrument and must not be presented as a measured gain in student learning. The default learner proxy remains another limitation in the inputs to this design.')
